-- Passage rg5-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c240ec2e-cb02-59c4-bc33-a2a5fa086273', 'rg5-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-context-clues'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How a 3D Printer Builds$q$, $q$A regular printer puts ink on flat paper. A 3D printer does something very different. It builds solid objects that you can hold in your hand.

First, a person designs the object on a computer. The design is like a blueprint, a detailed plan that shows the exact size and shape of every part.

Next, the printer heats a thin strand of plastic until it becomes soft. A small nozzle squeezes out the warm plastic, much like frosting coming out of a decorating bag. The nozzle moves back and forth, laying down one thin layer at a time. Each new layer sits on top of the last one. As the plastic cools, it becomes rigid, so the object holds its shape.

Printing can be a tedious process. A small toy might take an hour, and a large model can take a whole day. Still, the results are worth the wait.

Today, 3D printers are versatile tools. People use them to make toy parts, spare knobs for ovens, models of bridges, and even shoes.$q$, 'draft')
