insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9bd03cc1-2186-54a1-b9a6-fc9ce87d99ca', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Rivers of Ice$q$, $q$A glacier is a huge body of ice that moves very slowly over land. It forms in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow piles up and presses down on the layers below. Over time, the bottom layers turn into thick, solid ice.

When a glacier grows heavy enough, it begins to slide downhill. Most glaciers move only a few inches or feet each day. That is too slow for a person to notice by watching. Scientists track the movement by placing markers on the ice and measuring them again weeks later.

As a glacier moves, it acts like a giant scraper. Rocks frozen into the bottom of the ice grind against the ground. They leave long scratches on the bedrock below. These scratches all point in the same direction, the direction the ice traveled.

Glaciers also change the shape of whole valleys. A river usually cuts a narrow valley shaped like the letter V. A glacier is much wider, so it carves a broad valley shaped like the letter U. Some U-shaped valleys are found in places that have no ice today.

When a glacier melts, it drops the rocks and soil it was carrying. Sometimes a single boulder ends up sitting alone in a field, far from any rock like it. Geologists call these boulders erratics, because they seem to have wandered away from home.$q$, 'draft')
