-- Passage rg5-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('785b7f77-91f6-5664-a58c-da3bfae33c0a', 'rg5-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-evidence-inference'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Our Class Needs a Weather Station$q$, $q$Our school has a science lab, a row of tomato plants and a fish tank. What it does not have is a weather station. I believe every fifth-grade class should build and run one.

A simple station does not cost much. It needs a rain gauge, a thermometer that records the high and low each day, and a wind vane. Students can even build the rain gauge from a clear plastic bottle and a ruler.

A weather station would turn science class into real research. Instead of reading about rainfall in a textbook, students would measure it themselves. They would record numbers every morning and look for patterns over the season. Did the coldest mornings come after clear nights? Did the wind change before a storm? Questions like these make students think like scientists.

Some people worry that the equipment would be broken or forgotten. That is a fair concern. However, classes could take turns as station keepers, one week at a time. A sign-up chart would make sure someone checks the station every school day.

The data could help the whole school, too. The garden club could check the rain gauge before deciding whether to water the tomatoes. Coaches could look at the wind and temperature before outdoor practice. A weather station is a small project that would teach big lessons.$q$, 'draft')
