  ('ea190309-c81f-53aa-b5cc-5583b0b5eeb6', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In paragraph 5, the author uses the saying "where there is a will, there is a way." How does this proverb fit the octopus?$q$, $q$[{"text":"An octopus gives up quickly when a jar lid is too tight.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"An octopus always waits for people to feed it each day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"An octopus follows the same path in its tank every night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"When an octopus wants food, it finds a clever way to get it.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$When an octopus wants food, it finds a clever way to get it.$q$, $q$The proverb means that wanting something badly enough helps you find a way, and the octopuses "unscrew jar lids to reach food." The text shows them solving the problem, not giving up.$q$, 'draft', 'be6f451d-b89d-521b-9668-dcbabc3d4534')
