-- Passage rg5-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d596dcc2-6c8b-5890-bb6d-99d5a76dac15', 'rg5-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Riders of the Pony Express$q$, $q$In 1860, sending a letter across the United States was painfully slow. Mail could take weeks to travel from Missouri to California by stagecoach or ship. A new company had a bold idea. It would carry the mail on horseback, using a long chain of riders.

This service was called the Pony Express. A rider galloped about ten to fifteen miles to a station. There he swapped his tired horse for a fresh one. After about seventy-five miles, a new rider took over. The mail pouch passed from hand to hand like a baton in a relay race.

The riders had to be light, brave and tough. They crossed deserts, mountains and rivers in all kinds of weather. For the company, time was money, so riders rarely stopped to rest. A letter could now cross the country in about ten days.

The Pony Express was a shooting star. It blazed across the sky for only about eighteen months. In 1861, telegraph lines finally stretched from coast to coast. Messages could now zip along the wire in minutes. Almost overnight, the riders were out of work.

Even so, people still remember the Pony Express. Its riders showed that a team working together can do something remarkable.$q$, 'draft')
