  ('ba44908d-95c7-5b19-8703-cf8e18723cc8', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$In paragraph 2, the author compares the charge in a cloud to "the static you feel after shuffling across a rug in socks." Why does the author use this simile?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To prove that static and lightning are exactly the same size.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"To warn readers never to walk on rugs during a storm.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To show that clouds are made of rugs and socks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"To help readers understand the charge using something familiar.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To help readers understand the charge using something familiar.$q$, $q$Most readers have felt a small shock from static, so the simile links the hard idea of an electric charge to an everyday feeling. The paragraph gives no warning about rugs.$q$, 'draft', '03e0eb7d-ec8f-5b7a-bb86-45fc728e4fcf')
