-- Passage rg5-figurative-language-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('52f07ced-071b-58b1-846c-9feb0fcfd543', 'rg5-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Frog That Would Not Jump$q$, $q$Every Thursday, the coding club met in Room 12. This month, Hana and her partner, Rafael, were building a simple computer game. In it, a little frog hopped across lily pads to reach a golden fly.

For two weeks, the work was a breeze. The frog moved, the lily pads floated, and the score went up. Then, one Thursday, the frog stopped jumping. It just sat on the first lily pad like a stubborn rock.

Rafael groaned. "We're back to square one," he said. Hana shook her head. "Not quite. Most of the game still works. We just have to find the mistake."

They read every line of code, one at a time. It felt like looking for a needle in a haystack. There were hundreds of lines, and the mistake could be hiding anywhere.

Finally, Hana spotted it. One tiny number was wrong. Instead of telling the frog to jump two spaces, the code told it to jump zero spaces. Rafael fixed the number and pressed start. The frog sprang across the pads and snapped up the fly.

Their teacher, Ms. Lindqvist, peeked over their shoulders. "Two heads are better than one," she said with a smile. Hana and Rafael gave each other a high five.$q$, 'draft')
