-- Passage rg5-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2d48b245-1550-5774-b39b-21c8eb7a747d', 'rg5-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Ships of the Desert$q$, $q$The desert is a tough place to live. Days are blazing hot, nights can be freezing cold, and water is as rare as snow in summer. Yet the camel is right at home there.

A camel's hump does not hold water, as many people think. Instead, it stores fat. When food is scarce, the camel's body uses that fat for energy. A camel with a plump hump is well fed. A camel with a floppy hump needs a good meal.

Camels can go many days without a drink. When they do find water, they can gulp a huge amount in just a few minutes. Their bodies save nearly every drop. A camel is a walking water tank.

Other features help too. Long, thick eyelashes protect a camel's eyes from blowing sand. Its wide, padded feet act like snowshoes, so it does not sink into soft dunes. Camels can even close their nostrils during a sandstorm.

For thousands of years, people have relied on camels to carry goods across deserts. Travelers have called the camel the "ship of the desert." Like a sturdy boat on the ocean, it carries heavy loads across a sea of sand.$q$, 'draft')
