   $q$The narrator says, "Of course, the vane had it backward." How does this sentence affect the way readers see the vane's bragging?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It lets readers know the truth before the vane does, so the bragging seems foolish.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It makes readers believe the vane until the very last sentence.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It tells readers that the vane will win against the storm.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that the windmill has explained the truth to the vane.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It lets readers know the truth before the vane does, so the bragging seems foolish.$q$, $q$The narrator explains that "the wind pushed the vane," while the vane still thinks it is in charge, so readers know more than the vane. The text says "no one had ever told it so," so the windmill did not explain anything.$q$, 'draft', 'd5a9b747-cc36-555e-a2af-7a2d9f362593')
