   $q$The narrator says, "To most people, the clicks sounded like rain on a roof. To Aunt Marisol, they were words." How does this description shape the story?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows that most people in town could read the code.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"It shows that Aunt Marisol did not like rainy weather.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows that the telegraph office had a leaky roof.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It shows reading the clicks is a rare skill, which makes Rosa's moment matter.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows reading the clicks is a rare skill, which makes Rosa's moment matter.$q$, $q$The contrast shows that most people hear only noise, so when Rosa hears words in Chapter 2, readers see why her skill is special. The comparison to rain is about sound, not a real leaky roof.$q$, 'draft', '189b3fb7-c10b-59e6-b29c-4d02a18c8b07')
