-- Passage rg5-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1570f586-3eec-53b2-8b0f-1c8c0c73012e', 'rg5-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Cities Plant Street Trees$q$, $q$Walk down a city street on a hot afternoon, and you may notice something. The blocks with tall trees feel much cooler than the blocks without them. That is one reason many cities are planting more trees along their sidewalks.

Trees cool a street in two ways. First, their leaves block sunlight before it can heat the pavement. Second, trees give off water through their leaves, which cools the air around them. On summer days, a shady street can be several degrees cooler than a sunny one.

Street trees also help when it rains. Their roots soak up water, and the soil around them holds even more. This means less rainwater rushes into storm drains all at once, so streets are less likely to flood.

Planting trees in a city is not always easy, though. Roots need room to grow, and they can crack sidewalks if they are squeezed. Young trees need regular watering for their first few years. Some cities ask neighbors to help by watering the trees in front of their homes.

With care, a single street tree can last for many decades. Over that time, it keeps cooling the block and soaking up rain for everyone who lives nearby.$q$, 'draft')
