-- Passage rg5-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0bb9fbf0-1a9f-58a8-963b-cccd5ba771fe', 'rg5-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Recess Should Come Before Lunch$q$, $q$At most schools, students eat lunch first and go to recess afterward. I believe schools should switch the order. Holding recess before lunch would help students eat better and learn more.

First, students would eat more of their food. When recess comes right after lunch, many students rush through their meal so they can get outside sooner. They often throw away half of their sandwich or skip their fruit. After playing, students come in hungry and ready to sit down and eat.

Second, the lunchroom would be calmer. Students who have already run around and played are less restless. They can sit, eat and talk quietly with friends instead of watching the clock.

Finally, students would return to class more ready to learn. After lunch, they would have had both exercise and a full meal. Teachers at one school that made the switch said their classes settled down faster in the afternoon.

Some people worry that switching the schedule would be hard to arrange. However, it usually means only moving the times on a schedule, not adding any extra minutes to the day. Changing the order is a small step that could make a big difference.$q$, 'draft')
