insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0ab52069-e020-5ea7-9743-a483e3f3275a', 'rg5-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Cutting Ice from Frozen Lakes$q$, $q$Text A: The Ice Harvest Season

Long before freezers, people cut ice from frozen lakes. In early winter, workers waited until the ice was about a foot thick. Next, horses pulled sharp plows across the lake to mark a grid of lines. Then workers sawed along the lines to free large blocks. After that, they floated the blocks down a channel of open water to the shore. Finally, the blocks were packed in sawdust inside tall wooden icehouses. There, the ice could last until the middle of summer.

Text B: Why the Ice Harvest Ended

For many years, lake ice was a big business. Then electric refrigerators were invented. Because families could now make cold air at home, they no longer needed ice deliveries. As a result, fewer people bought lake ice each year. Icehouses stood empty, and many were torn down. Another cause was cleanliness. Factories could make ice from clean water, so stores stopped trusting ice from lakes. Because of these changes, the ice harvest slowly disappeared.$q$, 'draft')
