insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('495a642f-79f5-570c-b631-187720e12905', 'rg5-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Two Kinds of Bridges$q$, $q$Text A: Arch or Suspension?

Arch bridges and suspension bridges both carry people over rivers and valleys. However, they hold up weight in different ways. An arch bridge has a curved shape underneath. The curve pushes the weight outward and down into the ground at each end. A suspension bridge, on the other hand, hangs from thick cables. The cables stretch between tall towers and pull the road upward. Arch bridges are usually made of stone or concrete and work best for short spans. In contrast, suspension bridges can stretch across very wide spaces.

Text B: Building the Carver Creek Bridge

In the spring of one year, the town of Carver Creek began a new footbridge. First, workers poured concrete bases on both banks of the creek. During the summer, they built a wooden frame shaped like a curve. Next, they laid stone blocks along the frame, one row at a time. In the fall, the last block, called the keystone, was set at the very top. Only then did workers remove the wooden frame. By winter, students were walking across the finished arch bridge to school.$q$, 'draft')
