-- Passage rg5-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0c19487a-7c15-55c6-8ff4-d8a703d36b0f', 'rg5-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Fog Over the Harbor$q$, $q$Text A: How Fog Forms

Air always holds some water in the form of an invisible gas called water vapor. Warm air can hold more water vapor than cool air. When moist air cools down, it cannot hold all of its water vapor anymore. As a result, the extra vapor turns into tiny drops of liquid water. These drops float in the air near the ground, and we see them as fog. That is why fog often forms on clear nights, when the ground cools quickly.

Text B: The Foghorn Problem in Port Alder

The harbor town of Port Alder gets thick fog almost every summer morning. For years, boat captains could not see the rocks near the harbor entrance. Several boats scraped their hulls. At first, the town built a loud foghorn, but it woke up everyone in town before sunrise. Neighbors complained. So the town tried a new idea. It placed floating lights on the rocks and gave each boat a radio signal to follow. Now captains can find their way safely, and the town sleeps quietly.$q$, 'draft')
