-- Passage rg5-theme-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('33545167-b92b-5b90-9991-d84dc4cb5d6f', 'rg5-theme-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-theme-summary'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Bird Count$q$, $q$Every winter, the nature center held a bird count. Volunteers sat at windows and counted every bird they saw at the feeders for one hour. This year, Nadia was old enough to help.

She sat beside a tall man named Mr. Okafor with a clipboard on her lap. She counted chickadees, sparrows and two bright red cardinals. She checked each one carefully.

Near the end of the hour, a boy at the next window shouted, "A snowy owl! In the pine tree!"

Everyone rushed over. Nadia pressed her face to the glass. She saw a white shape in the branches, but it did not move. When the wind shook the tree, the shape fell off. It was a clump of snow.

Later, the volunteers read their lists aloud. Two people said they had seen the snowy owl. They wanted to add it to the count. A snowy owl would be the first one ever recorded at the center.

Nadia's cheeks felt hot. She raised her hand. "I think it was snow," she said softly. "I saw it fall."

The room went quiet. Then Mr. Okafor nodded. "Scientists use these counts to learn how birds are doing," he said. "A wrong number is worse than a boring one."

The owl was left off the list. On the way out, Mr. Okafor handed Nadia her own clipboard for next year.$q$, 'draft')
