insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('47912ddf-e8ea-5c50-8583-a80ea6b13e95', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Every Student Should Learn to Type$q$, $q$Many students today write most of their schoolwork on computers, yet few are ever taught how to type properly. Our school should teach keyboarding to every student starting in third grade.

First, typing well saves time. When students hunt for each key with one or two fingers, writing a single paragraph can take a long time. Mr. Adeyemi's sixth-grade class tested this. Students who had practiced typing with all ten fingers wrote about twice as many words in ten minutes as students who had not.

Second, fast typing lets students focus on their ideas. When a student has to search for every letter, it is easy to lose track of a thought. Several students in Mr. Adeyemi's class said that once typing became easier, they could think about what to say instead of where the keys were.

Third, typing is a skill students will use for the rest of their lives. Adults type emails, reports and messages every day in many kinds of jobs.

Students who learn to type will also become the best writers in the country.

Some teachers worry that typing will replace handwriting. But schools can teach both. A short typing lesson two times a week would leave plenty of time for handwriting practice.

Others say students can just learn to type at home. However, not every home has a computer, and without lessons, many students build slow habits that are hard to break.

Typing is a basic tool for learning. Every student deserves the chance to master it.$q$, 'draft')
